## 3. Adding and Removing Nodes

### Q: What happens when you add a new server to the ring?

Only keys in the arc between the new server and the previous server (counter-clockwise) need to move — and they move from the server that was previously clockwise-next.

**Example:** Ring with DB1(0), DB2(25), DB3(50), DB4(75). Add DB5 at position 90.
- Only keys hashing to positions 76–90 need to move
- They were previously owned by DB1 (the next clockwise server from 75)
- Now they belong to DB5
- All other keys stay put

Result: ~15% of total data moves instead of ~75%+ with modulo hashing.

### Q: What happens when a server goes down?

Only the keys that were on the failed server need to move, and they all go to the next clockwise server.

**Example:** DB2 (position 25) fails.
- Keys at positions 1–25 were owned by DB2
- They now map to DB3 (position 50), the next clockwise server
- DB1, DB3, DB4 keys are completely unaffected

| Operation | Modulo hashing | Consistent hashing |
|-----------|---------------|-------------------|
| Add 1 node to 4-node cluster | ~75% keys move | ~20% keys move (only from one neighbor) |
| Remove 1 node from 4-node cluster | ~75% keys move | ~25% keys move (only to one neighbor) |

