## 4. Virtual Nodes

### Q: What problem do virtual nodes solve?

Basic consistent hashing has a load imbalance problem. When a node goes down, ALL its keys transfer to a single neighbor. That neighbor now has 2x the load while others are unchanged.

Similarly, with few physical nodes, the arcs on the ring can be very uneven, leading to some servers holding far more data than others.

### Q: How do virtual nodes work?

Instead of placing each physical server at one position on the ring, you place it at **many** positions by hashing variations of its name:

```
hash("DB1-vn1") → position 20
hash("DB1-vn2") → position 35
hash("DB1-vn3") → position 65
hash("DB1-vn4") → position 88
```

All these virtual positions map back to the same physical DB1. You do this for every server, so virtual nodes from different physical servers are naturally intermixed around the ring.

### Q: How do virtual nodes improve failure handling?

When DB2 fails and it had virtual nodes at positions 10, 40, 70, 90:
- Keys from "DB2-vn1" (position 10) redistribute to whichever server's virtual node is next clockwise — maybe DB3
- Keys from "DB2-vn2" (position 40) redistribute to DB1
- Keys from "DB2-vn3" (position 70) redistribute to DB4
- Keys from "DB2-vn4" (position 90) redistribute to DB1

The failed server's load spreads **evenly across all remaining servers** instead of dumping onto a single neighbor.

### Q: How do virtual nodes help when adding a server?

Without virtual nodes, a new server only takes load from its single clockwise neighbor. With virtual nodes, the new server's virtual positions are spread around the ring, absorbing small chunks of data from multiple existing nodes — giving a balanced cluster from the start.

### Q: How many virtual nodes should you use?

More virtual nodes = more even distribution, but more memory for the ring metadata. In practice:
- 100–200 virtual nodes per physical server is common
- Cassandra uses 256 tokens (virtual nodes) per node by default
- Diminishing returns beyond a few hundred

