## 7. Real-World Systems

### Q: Which major systems use consistent hashing?

| System | How it uses consistent hashing |
|--------|-------------------------------|
| **Apache Cassandra** | Distributes data across the ring. Each node owns a token range. Uses virtual nodes (vnodes) by default. |
| **Amazon DynamoDB** | Partition placement under the hood. Combined with replication across AZs. |
| **CDNs (Akamai, etc.)** | Determines which edge server caches specific content. Minimizes cache invalidation when servers are added/removed. |
| **Memcached clients** | Client-side consistent hashing to distribute cache keys across a pool of memcached servers. |

### Q: Do all distributed systems use consistent hashing?

No. **Redis Cluster** uses a fixed hash slot approach:
- Divides the key space into exactly 16,384 slots
- Uses `CRC16(key) mod 16384` to assign keys to slots
- Assigns ranges of slots to nodes

| Approach | Consistent hashing | Fixed hash slots (Redis) |
|----------|-------------------|------------------------|
| Complexity | Moderate (ring, virtual nodes) | Simpler (just slot ranges) |
| Rebalancing | Automatic minimal movement | Requires coordination to move slot ranges |
| Flexibility | Elastic — nodes join/leave smoothly | More manual — resharding is explicit |
| When to use | Large clusters, frequent scaling | Smaller clusters, infrequent topology changes |

