## Interview-Ready One-Liners

### "Why not just use modulo hashing?"
> "Modulo hashing causes nearly all keys to remap when you add or remove a single node. Consistent hashing bounds the movement to only the keys in the affected arc — roughly 1/N of total data."

### "How does consistent hashing work?"
> "You place both servers and keys on a circular hash space. Each key walks clockwise to its nearest server. Adding or removing a server only disturbs keys in one arc of the ring."

### "What are virtual nodes?"
> "Instead of one ring position per server, you hash each server to many positions. This intermixes servers around the ring so when one fails, its load distributes evenly across all survivors instead of piling onto one neighbor."

### "How do you handle hot keys?"
> "Consistent hashing distributes keys evenly but not traffic. For hot keys, use read replicas to spread reads, or key salting to split one hot key into multiple keys that hash to different nodes."

### "Does data move when a node fails?"
> "In practice, no — production systems replicate data to multiple nodes. When one fails, a replica is promoted. Data movement only happens during planned scaling to restore replication factor."

